#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/id #uuid "fe39d0a2-20cd-5d77-8801-c1a8575706e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10876" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi ) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "426cd4bd-909d-527f-a35c-9bb10ef3e734" :question/math-academy-id "e-10877" :question/problem "Suppose that a pool has a small hole and that water is leaking out at a rate of $t^{1/3}$ gallons per minute, where $t$ is the time in minutes. If at time $t = 0$ the pool contains $100$ gallons of water, how many gallons of water will remain in the pool after $t$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t) = -t^{1/3}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) \t\t\t= F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-{\\int }_{0}^{T}{t}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3{t}^{4/3}}{4}{|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3(T)^{4/3}}{4}+\\frac{3(0)^{4/3}}{4} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{4/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "834e0019-f098-5b33-8579-2fae3f46ba78" :question/math-academy-id "e-10884" :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee816e61-64a8-5b72-a561-38bf9e8f40dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289439} {:answer/id #uuid "f32844d7-93c9-530e-8615-f21d082247b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289440} {:answer/id #uuid "b10c74bc-21b6-56b3-a04b-cc464e4be32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289441} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8d0ab7-1852-521f-94ab-b838c010d72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289442} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf20e6-65e4-5ce0-ba21-466f62593d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf20e6-65e4-5ce0-ba21-466f62593d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289443} :answer-field/id #uuid "3694a628-218c-5e32-a6d8-a475f9367072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86168165-44dd-55ce-89eb-7dc3a8b70b80" :question/math-academy-id "q-103770" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/749bafb0b977dc67bbf444edc6c51c884a55874655553671330462037c7f1e45.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/813a6e18ee953306699ffe65b5af99c9d44baa1d9e958572a6aad14b4e139f2c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-1,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-1,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+(4-\\pi )=10-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c34b16aa-d2c5-5016-9f5c-abee5ae43797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289446} {:answer/id #uuid "e43e8d72-f6c7-5e7e-94d4-123112f5a257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289447} {:answer/id #uuid "75b0011f-24d9-5a8b-a2c9-f0707eba97c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289448} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f99551-65a4-573a-820b-0959e5c738ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289449} {:answer/id #uuid "d85a0806-22c6-5853-b0cf-1d4f8afb9c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d85a0806-22c6-5853-b0cf-1d4f8afb9c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289450} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c492a7c-8675-56d4-a802-ad8695e484f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edaab010-508a-5396-9c9c-3e51a45f918f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103774" :question/problem "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{2}^{x}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(3)={\\int }_{2}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+{3}^{2})-({2}^{3}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(27+9)-(8+4) \\\\  & =36-12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e447f3-327c-5000-bc0c-2f468f1b4916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061259} {:answer/id #uuid "cb40830c-dba2-5d0e-bef7-3acad933cf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061260} {:answer/id #uuid "e67dd1ab-fd32-5d15-8b28-fe0183f10b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061261} {:answer/id #uuid "f5826fb7-6e44-5d8e-831b-ac6c510880bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061262} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3bd5ac-32f6-582f-9d3d-ab124c90c8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5826fb7-6e44-5d8e-831b-ac6c510880bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061262} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a56bac-fbb7-5704-9c39-8cfb62bd543a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abd361ea-0dd1-53a8-8717-46e052a5b559" :question/math-academy-id "q-155514" :question/problem "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(({3}^{3})^{1/2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8486beba-8d42-518a-979c-7dd6b7867abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061265} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad70ef7-dec3-5ae9-93eb-407457cd308a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061266} {:answer/id #uuid "e6946b81-7a63-5729-9c4a-726bb0c427bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061267} {:answer/id #uuid "20708854-07e6-5192-824b-dc067aefdb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061268} {:answer/id #uuid "2d45b30b-d37c-5d10-91fb-9616c18d8508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20708854-07e6-5192-824b-dc067aefdb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061268} :answer-field/id #uuid "713eec68-5f14-5e42-abae-f23854b8863e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acbc28aa-c46b-5e1e-aadd-c2c3d31e5dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-155515" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec7303bf-f66d-5093-8a11-47066678cbf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061271} {:answer/id #uuid "faf288ef-eade-51ef-8cf4-44a6a6030e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061272} {:answer/id #uuid "fc59128c-d17b-539d-83c3-ca493076ae1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061273} {:answer/id #uuid "38e3e272-f835-5a45-82a0-afc0e6fd597d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061274} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6f769e-5af5-5ed5-9509-5e6a49063d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec7303bf-f66d-5093-8a11-47066678cbf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061271} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c734b4-fbd3-52c2-abe2-fff435d73e73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95e7c02f-cf4f-50e1-895d-228e0f024189" :question/math-academy-id "q-155717" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/21c86a5542f6bac4cd53c9d64d7c24a90ea6e901bd58e0204f38a91c2835e3aa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/b14fbfe336923b44e4e4f3660bef7c6be97ada82ea07c4523a3f25f2905f4fd6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,3],$  for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square.  This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+9=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f05b44e-fe26-5814-826e-fc2d3401ac19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - \\frac{9}{4}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061277} {:answer/id #uuid "8f70ebae-5b59-5487-9ea6-10d7512be892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17 - \\frac{3}{2}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061278} {:answer/id #uuid "2cadd2c6-c9bf-52a7-97d3-c6e0d5b7766a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 - \\frac{9}{4}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061279} {:answer/id #uuid "43fcfb59-570a-59cb-a444-ebebcfa208f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 - \\frac{3}{2}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061280} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdae429-9823-56d3-bfee-78341f6798de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 - \\frac{9}{2}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f05b44e-fe26-5814-826e-fc2d3401ac19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - \\frac{9}{4}π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7eb737-bd76-5c29-9e4a-0149b72dea64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69bdc4f2-1ce2-56ea-a305-35d352a03390" :question/math-academy-id "q-155948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/d41d681a2f0e7a32400d863e60294efc9864ff6245de0e36515986da656ddbd2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(5).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=5,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/7db30a3be837d9303ce4fca0b5a95466a74fe53f1ce81e6fa0febe2ef00625b5.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,5],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(5)={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,5],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+(12-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi ) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{9}{4}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adcfd6c-b637-558e-a7e5-45b52d0ecf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289453} {:answer/id #uuid "b6045a1b-47db-57e3-9dbb-761ca94a3938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289454} {:answer/id #uuid "e82c20a1-3437-5fdd-aa50-081bba41e3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289455} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4fe3f6-a5aa-57e8-a2b8-6b502c1458f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289456} {:answer/id #uuid "59cae77d-d8dd-5f83-ae0a-319dcffbe847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e82c20a1-3437-5fdd-aa50-081bba41e3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289455} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c722781-d362-5cea-ac9a-80896902ad60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74194f45-8c0e-5305-bf6c-f0844fbf03f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-156193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a30e20e-c573-57eb-8007-3f9e1015ff81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = t^{3/2} - t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061794} {:answer/id #uuid "bee34a5f-f885-5441-a78f-383c56942970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = 2t^{3/2} + t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061795} {:answer/id #uuid "46ecac12-de82-5cc6-b393-c7da6a336a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = 3t^{3/2} - t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061796} {:answer/id #uuid "47124b55-fd92-507c-81a8-aefdc9547df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = \\frac{2t^{3/2}}{3} + t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061797} {:answer/id #uuid "482d9a7e-3829-5075-8736-0f532405e96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = 2t^{3/2} - t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482d9a7e-3829-5075-8736-0f532405e96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t) = 2t^{3/2} - t^{2} + 1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061798} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d318cec-38b8-5ede-829d-63b30564b929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef1fb432-e4fe-5f9e-a680-f538f3e2c97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2370" :question/problem "A pumping station adds sand to Sandy Beach at a rate of $3\\sqrt{t}$ cubic yards per hour. The tide removes the sand from the beach at a rate of $2t$ cubic yards per hour. If at time $t=0$ the beach contains $1\\,500$ cubic yards of sand, write an expression for $A(t),$ the total number of cubic yards of sand on the beach at any time $t>0.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=3\\sqrt{t}-2t$ be the rate at which the volume of sand at Sandy Beach is changing. Note that we subtract $2t$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $A(T)$ be the volume of sand on the beach at any time after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3\\sqrt{t}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{(2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =1\\,500+2{T}^{3/2}-{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have \n\n$$\nA(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a84e7ca6-d622-5cb4-b4e9-d52c45896966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289460} {:answer/id #uuid "c9863bf7-30b6-51f0-9463-f99167271cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9863bf7-30b6-51f0-9463-f99167271cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289461} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5c933c-8444-5239-8eed-cff8e217dd50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dad0d7cd-1bc8-5127-af3b-af2f860eb258" :question/math-academy-id "q-346529" :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4}-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20fc6aa-6bcd-5099-8a8b-6fa48073d8c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+4t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20fc6aa-6bcd-5099-8a8b-6fa48073d8c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+4t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289464} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5e08674-569d-565a-b219-d159ce540e9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b461f9e1-3b9b-50b8-9dd8-f66f35924c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346569" :question/problem "Fuel is being pumped into a storage tank at a rate of $8t$ gallons per hour. If at time $t=0$ the tank already contains $12$ gallons of fuel, write an expression for $V(t),$ the total amount of fuel in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=8t$ represent the rate at which fuel enters the tank, and let $V(T)$ be the total amount of fuel in the tank after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nV(T)=V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(T) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+{\\int }_{0}^{T}8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={12+(4{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =12+4{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nV(t)=12+4{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd239d4-ae29-5444-86bc-e19f304be084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289467} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ee719-ac45-5fcf-aba5-15ea999e1ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ee719-ac45-5fcf-aba5-15ea999e1ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ac806a-e453-5c07-8432-b0b5058d7f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c74918-41fb-5354-ad06-5491e1e76191" :question/math-academy-id "q-346576" :question/problem "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b123d80-346c-5bf8-ae68-fa6c8422d87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b123d80-346c-5bf8-ae68-fa6c8422d87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289471} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d69349-ea38-5995-839b-f9c0b3bef35d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1e6a564-2a5c-5444-bc19-f789dc3cf80b" :question/math-academy-id "q-346579" :question/problem "The total mass, in kilograms, of compost produced $t$ days after a process begins increases at a rate of $7{e}^{t}.$ If, at $t=0$ days, the process had already produced $5$ kilograms of compost, how much compost will the process have produced after $2$ days?\n\nAfter $2$ days, the process has produced\n{{field-1}} kilograms of compost." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=7{e}^{t}$ be the rate at which the amount of compost produced is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of compost produced $T$ days after the process begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{\\int }_{0}^{T}7{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =5+(7{e}^{T})-(7{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5+7{e}^{T}-7 \\\\  & =7{e}^{T}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of compost produced $T=2$ days after the process begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=5+7{e}^{2}-7=7{e}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ days, the process has produced $7{e}^{2}-2$ kilograms of compost."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90c4015-d4d7-5b52-9a4d-cc7fbb59e5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t+t^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289474} {:answer/id #uuid "6b416168-ef46-5d46-8cf9-4d5f9d298d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t+\\frac{2}{5}t^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b416168-ef46-5d46-8cf9-4d5f9d298d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t+\\frac{2}{5}t^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289475} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2f6dc6-b95f-5946-9293-82ebda6afa9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e41bc59-4a5e-5a99-90ca-7ed4621f9292" :question/math-academy-id "q-346582" :question/problem "An engineer started tracking the amount of electric charge stored in a battery. The initial charge was $75$ coulombs, and the charging and discharging rates are given by $6+{t}^{3/2}$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of hours since the beginning of the observation.  \n\nFind an expression for the charge $C(t)$ stored in the battery at any time $t>0.$\n\n$C(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $c(t)$ be the rate of change of the charge. According to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)={{6+{t}^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{charge decrease}}=5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ represent the charge stored $T$ hours after $2015.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+(5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =75+5T+\\frac{2}{5}{T}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nC(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce75730f-0abd-5a62-a8da-af8f643267ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289478} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eeed81-b601-5d36-8662-1b29afc11416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289479} {:answer/id #uuid "bfafa281-9e49-5624-a306-02521053ef2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289480} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c6b0b-b7cb-5aa7-b48a-fca8bc5707f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289481} {:answer/id #uuid "eae11aa8-42f4-5303-ad12-23a053ac5d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1eeed81-b601-5d36-8662-1b29afc11416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae626f66-c783-5ca3-aebd-a60b3d9d70b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e894ab8-304a-5125-a424-65cae6e46215" :question/math-academy-id "q-49309" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010573/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8d98fe-9bb6-54d7-98ee-2f2dde61b0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289485} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb78485-64bf-5bbb-8808-0611f9ff7b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289486} {:answer/id #uuid "30a44ec9-a864-559f-ae64-e2686506593b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289487} {:answer/id #uuid "9eef1c54-fcc4-5fcd-a8db-9cfd00c199f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289488} {:answer/id #uuid "ff38cf5c-a76f-545b-b645-6113b2c9a1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eef1c54-fcc4-5fcd-a8db-9cfd00c199f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289488} :answer-field/id #uuid "71719c70-b4fe-540f-a50a-b07457b6f5a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63fdf8a4-58cd-5de1-934a-39eee71dc649" :question/math-academy-id "q-51217" :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
